(a) A header file (.h) is a file of declarations (function prototypes, macros, type definitions and constants) that is copied into a program by the preprocessor directive #include before compilation, e.g. #include <stdio.h>. It lets the compiler check calls to functions defined elsewhere.
A library function is a ready-made, pre-compiled function supplied with the compiler in the standard library; its prototype is in a header file and its code is linked in at link time. The programmer calls it without writing it, e.g. printf(), sqrt().

| Header file | Purpose | Example library functions |
|---|---|---|
| stdio.h | Standard input/output | printf(), scanf(), fgets(), fopen() |
| string.h | String handling | strlen(), strcpy(), strcmp(), strcat() |
| math.h | Mathematics | sqrt(), pow(), sin() |
| ctype.h | Character tests and conversion | isupper(), tolower(), toupper(), isdigit() |
| stdlib.h | Utilities, memory | malloc(), free(), atoi(), exit() |

(b) Program

