Definition: Command-line arguments are values typed after the program name when it is launched from a terminal; C receives them through the parameters of main: int main(int argc, char *argv[]).

- argc (argument count): the number of arguments, INCLUDING the program name itself.
- argv (argument vector): an array of strings; argv[0] is the program name, argv[1]..argv[argc-1] are the user arguments, and argv[argc] is NULL. Each is a string, so a numeric argument must be converted with atoi/strtol (<stdlib.h>).

Example (compiled and run with gcc):
#include <stdio.h>
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    printf("argc=%d\n", argc);
    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) printf("argv[%d]=%s\n", i, argv[i]);
    return 0;
}
Run as: ./prog alpha beta gamma
Output:
argc=4
argv[0]=./prog
argv[1]=alpha
argv[2]=beta
argv[3]=gamma

Use: command-line arguments let one program behave differently without recompiling - e.g. passing a filename to process, like ./wordcount report.txt. Advantage: flexible, scriptable; limitation: all input arrives as text and must be validated and converted.

