model_answer_en:
Program (compiled and run with gcc):
#include <stdio.h>
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc < 2) { printf("Usage: %s <file>\n", argv[0]); return 1; }
    FILE *fp = fopen(argv[1], "r");
    if (fp == NULL) { perror(argv[1]); return 1; }
    int c, lines = 0, words = 0, chars = 0, inWord = 0;
    while ((c = fgetc(fp)) != EOF) {
        chars++;
        if (c == '\n') lines++;
        if (c == ' ' || c == '\n' || c == '\t') inWord = 0;
        else if (!inWord) { inWord = 1; words++; }
    }
    fclose(fp);
    printf("lines=%d words=%d chars=%d\n", lines, words, chars);
    return 0;
}

For an input file wc_in.txt containing:
hello world foo
second line here

Run as ./wc wc_in.txt, the actual output (verified) is:
lines=2 words=6 chars=33

Notes: argc < 2 handles a missing argument; perror(argv[1]) reports why the open failed (using errno), satisfying the error-handling requirement. getchar-style reading with fgetc returns int so EOF is detected correctly; a word is counted on each transition from whitespace into a non-space character.

