model_answer_en:
Program (compiled and run with gcc):
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdarg.h>
double average(int n, ...) {
    va_list args; va_start(args, n);
    double sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += va_arg(args, int);
    va_end(args);
    return n ? sum / n : 0;
}
int main(void) {
    printf("avg=%.2f\n", average(5, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50));
    printf("avg=%.2f\n", average(3, 7, 8, 9));
    return 0;
}

Actual output (verified):
avg=30.00
avg=8.00

Working: n is the count; va_start initialises the list after n, va_arg(args, int) pulls each integer, and va_end cleans up. (10+20+30+40+50)/5 = 150/5 = 30.00 and (7+8+9)/3 = 24/3 = 8.00. The n ? sum/n : 0 guard avoids division by zero when no values are passed.

